\fs96 \cf2 Mojo language basics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This page provides an overview of the Mojo language.\
If you know Python, then a lot of Mojo code will look familiar. However, Mojo incorporates features like static type checking, memory safety, next-generation compiler technologies, and more. As such, Mojo also has a lot in common with languages like C++ and Rust.\
If you prefer to learn by doing, follow the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/get-started/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Get started with Mojo}} tutorial.\
On this page, we'll introduce the essential Mojo syntax, so you can start coding quickly and understand other Mojo code you encounter. Subsequent sections in the Mojo Manual dive deeper into these topics, and links are provided below as appropriate.\
Let's get started! \uc0\u55357 \u56613 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 Mojo is a young language and there are many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/roadmap/"}}{\fldrslt \ul features still missing}}. As such, Mojo is currently not meant for beginners. Even this basics section assumes some programming experience. However, throughout the Mojo Manual, we try not to assume experience with any particular language.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Hello world\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Here's the traditional "Hello world" program in Mojo:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Hello, world!"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Every Mojo program must include a function named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 main()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the entry point. We'll talk more about functions soon, but for now it's enough to know that you can write 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 def main():
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  followed by an indented function body.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 print()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement does what you'd expect, printing its arguments to the standard output.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, you can declare a variable by simply assigning a value to a new named variable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     x \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 10\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     y \cf5 =\cf14  x \cf5 *\cf14  x\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 y\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also explicitly declare variables with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  x \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 10\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When declaring a variable with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can also declare a variable type, with or without an assignment:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  x\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 10\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  \cf15 sum\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf15 sum\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  x \cf5 +\cf14  x\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Both implicitly declared and explicitly declared variables are statically typed: that is, the type is set at compile time, and doesn't change at runtime. If you don't specify a type, Mojo uses the type of the first value assigned to the variable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 x \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 10\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 x \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "Foo"\cf14  \cf6 # Error: Cannot convert "StringLiteral" value to "Int"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more details, see the page about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/variables/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 variables}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Blocks and statements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Code blocks such as functions, conditions, and loops are defined with a colon followed by indented lines. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 loop\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 for\cf14  x \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf20 5\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 if\cf14  x \cf5 %\cf14  \cf20 2\cf14  \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf20 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf5 print(\cf14 x\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can use any number of spaces or tabs for your indentation (we prefer 4 spaces).\
All code statements in Mojo end with a newline. However, statements can span multiple lines if you indent the following lines. For example, this long string spans two lines:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 print_line\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     long_text \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "This is a long line of text that is a lot easier to read if"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13                 \cf16 " it is broken up across two lines instead of one long line."\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 long_text\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And you can chain function calls across lines:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 print_hello\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     text \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 ","\cf5 .\cf14 join\cf5 (\cf16 "Hello"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 " world!"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 text\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more information on loops and conditional statements, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/control-flow/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Control flow}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You define Mojo functions with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. For example, the following uses the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to define a function named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 greet
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that requires a single 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument and returns a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 greet\cf5 (\cf14 name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 )\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  String\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  \cf16 "Hello, "\cf14  \cf5 +\cf14  name \cf5 +\cf14  \cf16 "!"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Code comments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can create a one-line comment using the hash 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 #
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  symbol:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # This is a comment. The Mojo compiler ignores this line.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Comments may also follow some code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  message \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "Hello, World!"\cf14  \cf6 # This is also a valid comment\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 API documentation comments are enclosed in triple quotes. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 print\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf16 """Prints a string.\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \cb13     Args:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf16 \cb13         x: The string to print.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf16 \cb13     """\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Documenting your code with these kinds of comments (known as "docstrings") is a topic we've yet to fully specify, but you can generate an API reference from docstrings using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/doc/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 \ul \ulc5 mojo doc
\fs32 \cb1  command}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 Technically, docstrings aren't comments, they're a special use of Mojo's syntax for multi-line string literals. For details, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/#string-literals"}}{\fldrslt \ul String literals}} in the page on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Types}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Structs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can build high-level abstractions for types (or "objects") as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 struct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 struct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in Mojo is similar to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 class
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in Python: they both support methods, fields, operator overloading, decorators for metaprogramming, and so on. However, Mojo structs are completely static\'97they are bound at compile-time, so they do not allow dynamic dispatch or any runtime changes to the structure. (Mojo will also support Python-style classes in the future.)\
For example, here's a basic struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 (\cf14 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 first \cf5 =\cf14  first\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 second \cf5 =\cf14  second\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 *,\cf14  copy\cf5 :\cf14  Self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 first \cf5 =\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 first\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 second \cf5 =\cf14  copy\cf5 .\cf14 second\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 dump\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 first\cf5 ,\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 second\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And here's how you can use it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_mypair\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  mine \cf5 =\cf14  MyPair\cf5 (\cf20 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     mine\cf5 .\cf14 dump\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct contains two special methods, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the constructor, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 __init__(out self, *, copy: Self)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the copy constructor. Lifecycle methods like this control how a struct is created, copied, moved, and destroyed.\
For most simple types, you don't need to write the lifecycle methods. You can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 @fieldwise_init
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator to generate the boilerplate field-wise initializer for you, and Mojo will synthesize copy and move constructors if you ask for them with trait conformance. So the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct can be simplified to this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 (\cf14 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 dump\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 self\cf5 .\cf14 first\cf5 ,\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 second\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more details, see the page about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 structs}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Traits\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A trait is like a template of characteristics for a struct. If you want to create a struct with the characteristics defined in a trait, you must implement each characteristic (such as each method). Each characteristic in a trait is a "requirement" for the struct, and when your struct implements all of the requirements, it's said to "conform" to the trait.\
Using traits allows you to write generic functions that can accept any type that conforms to a trait, rather than accept only specific types.\
For example, here's how you can create a trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 trait\cf14  SomeTrait\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 required_method\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  x\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The three dots following the method signature are Mojo syntax indicating that the method is not implemented.\
Here's a struct that conforms to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 SomeTrait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 SomeStruct\cf5 (\cf14 SomeTrait\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 required_method\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  x\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "hello traits"\cf5 ,\cf14  x\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Then, here's a function that uses the trait as an argument type (instead of the struct type):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  fun_with_traits\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 :\cf14  SomeTrait\cf5 ](\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  T\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     x\cf5 .\cf14 required_method\cf5 (\cf20 42\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 use_trait_function\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  thing \cf5 =\cf14  SomeStruct\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     fun_with_traits\cf5 (\cf14 thing\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You'll see traits used in a lot of APIs provided by Mojo's standard library. For example, Mojo's collection types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can store any type that conforms to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. You can specify the type when you create a collection:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 my_list \cf5 =\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 Float64\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 You're probably wondering about the square brackets on 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 fun_with_traits()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 . These aren't function arguments (which go in parentheses); these are function parameters, which we'll explain next.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Without traits, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 fun_with_traits()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  would have to declare a specific type that implements 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 required_method()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 SomeStruct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (but then the function would accept only that type). With traits, the function can accept any type for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as long as it conforms to (it "implements") 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 SomeTrait
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Thus, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 fun_with_traits()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is known as a "generic function" because it accepts a generalized type instead of a specific type.\
For more details, see the page about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 traits}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Parameterization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Mojo, a parameter is a compile-time variable that becomes a runtime constant, and it's declared in square brackets on a function or struct. Parameters allow for compile-time metaprogramming, which means you can generate or modify code at compile time.\
Many other languages use "parameter" and "argument" interchangeably, so be aware that when we say things like "parameter" and "parametric function," we're talking about these compile-time parameters. Whereas, a function "argument" is a runtime value that's declared in parentheses.\
Parameterization is a complex topic that's covered in much more detail in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Metaprogramming}} section, but we want to break the ice just a little bit here. To get you started, let's look at a parametric function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  repeat\cf5 [\cf14 count\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ](\cf14 msg\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 )\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # evaluate the following for loop at compile time\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 for\cf14  i \cf5 in\cf14  \cf15 range\cf5 (\cf14 count\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 msg\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This function has one parameter of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and one argument of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . To call the function, you need to specify both the parameter and the argument:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 call_repeat\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     repeat\cf5 [\cf20 3\cf5 ](\cf16 "Hello"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Prints "Hello" 3 times\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 By specifying 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 count
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as a parameter, the Mojo compiler is able to optimize the function because this value is guaranteed to not change at runtime. And the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword in the code tells the compiler to evaluate the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop at compile time, not runtime.\
The compiler effectively generates a unique version of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 repeat()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function that repeats the message only 3 times. This makes the code more performant because there's less to compute at runtime.\
Similarly, you can define a struct with parameters, which effectively allows you to define variants of that type at compile-time, depending on the parameter values.\
For more detail on parameters, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Metaprogramming}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Python integration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo supports the ability to import Python modules as-is, so you can leverage existing Python code right away.\
For example, here's how you can import and use NumPy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  np \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf14 import_module\cf5 (\cf16 "numpy"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  ar \cf5 =\cf14  np\cf5 .\cf14 arange\cf5 (\cf20 15\cf5 ).\cf14 reshape\cf5 (\cf20 3\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 5\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 ar\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 ar\cf5 .\cf14 shape\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You must have the Python module (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 numpy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) installed in the environment where you're using Mojo.\
For more details, see the page on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/python/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Python integration}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

